---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import RobotWarsGame from '../../../components/RobotWarsGame.astro';
import { DRIVES, WEAPONS, BODIES, BRAINS, ZONES, ZONE_IDS, PART_IDS, STRONG, WEAK, SPEED, DIR_MUL, matchup, terrain } from '../../../scripts/robot-wars/sim';
import { MELEE_BOTS, MELEE_LIMIT } from '../../../scripts/robot-wars/melee';
const TITLE = 'Robot Rumble Melee';
const cell = { strong: `💪 ×${STRONG}`, weak: `🛡️ ×${WEAK}`, even: '×1' } as const;
const sp = { fast: `🚀 ×${SPEED.fast}`, slow: `🐌 ×${SPEED.slow}`, medium: '×1' } as const;
---
<Base title={`${TITLE} · CrinkleCutSnV's playground`} description="Build a fighting robot, throw it into a five-robot free-for-all with four random challengers and see who's the last robot standing.">
  <section class="wrap game-page">
    <div class="title-row">
      <h1>{TITLE} 💥</h1>
      <a class="back" href="/">← All apps</a>
    </div>
    <p class="lede">Build a robot from four parts, then drop it into the arena with four random challengers. It’s every robot for itself and the fight runs itself. Last robot standing wins gold, 2nd gets silver and 3rd bronze. Want a one-on-one instead? Try <a href="/apps/robot-wars/">Robot Rumble</a>: your robot works in both.</p>
    <RobotWarsGame mode="melee" />
    <details class="how">
      <summary>How it works</summary>
      <p>{MELEE_BOTS} robots start spread evenly round a big arena, turned to a new spot every melee. They’re all fully autonomous: your choices are all made in the workshop. A melee lasts up to {MELEE_LIMIT} seconds. Placings go by who gets knocked out when: the first robot out finishes 5th, the next 4th, and so on. If the clock runs out, the robots still standing are ranked by how much health they have left. Knocked-out robots are towed to the nearest wall, still smoking. Each knockout is credited to whoever landed the last blow. You can watch at 1×, 2× or 4×.</p>

      <h3>The arena</h3>
      <p>Four zones of different ground (shuffled every melee) round a neutral paved plaza in the middle, which is the same for every drive. Walls split the zones from each other, so the only ways between them are a doorway through each wall or the plaza’s open corners. Each zone also has two pillars to hide behind.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Walls and pillars are solid.</b> Robots slide along them, and nobody can see or swing through them.</li>
        <li><b>Being slammed into a wall hurts</b> (a few points, credited to whoever sent you flying). Flippers and hammers are best at it: a hammer blow knocks its victim back too.</li>
        <li>Every robot finds its own way round the walls. Flankers take the long way round to come at you from behind, and tacticians lie in wait at the way into the zone that suits them.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Who picks on whom</h3>
      <p>Every robot keeps choosing a target, and its computer decides how. Nobody is out to get you in particular: the robots treat each other exactly as they treat you, and they don’t like ganging up on a robot that’s already being chased.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>{BRAINS.aggressive.name}:</b> goes for whoever’s nearest, and turns on anyone who’s just hit it.</li>
        <li><b>{BRAINS.conservative.name}:</b> hangs back and picks off the most battered robot it can find, especially one busy with someone else.</li>
        <li><b>{BRAINS.flanker.name}:</b> hunts robots that are busy with somebody else or facing the other way, then goes for the side or the back.</li>
        <li><b>{BRAINS.tactician.name}:</b> picks the fight its weapon wins and its body survives, on ground that suits its drive.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A robot will also take a swing at anyone else who wanders into reach while it’s chasing its target.</p>

      <h3>Weapons vs bodies</h3>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Weapon ↓ / body →</th>{PART_IDS.body.map((b) => <th>{BODIES[b].name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>{PART_IDS.weapon.map((w) => <tr><th>{WEAPONS[w].name}</th>{PART_IDS.body.map((b) => <td class={matchup(w, b)}>{cell[matchup(w, b)]}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3>Drives vs ground</h3>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Drive ↓ / zone →</th>{ZONE_IDS.map((z) => <th>{ZONES[z].name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>{PART_IDS.drive.map((d) => <tr><th>{DRIVES[d].name}</th>{ZONE_IDS.map((z) => <td class={terrain(d, z)}>{sp[terrain(d, z)]}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
      </table></div>

      <h3>Where you hit them</h3>
      <p>Hits to the front do normal damage, hits to the side do ×{DIR_MUL.side}, and hits from behind do ×{DIR_MUL.rear}. In a melee there’s always someone behind you.</p>

      <h3>Medals</h3>
      <p>Medals are for your best finish: gold for winning the melee, silver for 2nd and bronze for 3rd. With four random challengers, even a great robot only wins some of the time. That’s the game!</p>
    </details>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .game-page { padding-bottom: 2rem; }
  .title-row { display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.4rem); margin: .25rem 0 .4rem; }
  .lede { margin: 0 0 1rem; max-width: 70ch; line-height: 1.45; }
  .back { font-weight: 700; text-decoration: none; }
  .how { margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 76ch; line-height: 1.5; }
  .how summary { font-weight: 800; cursor: pointer; }
  .how h3 { margin: 1.1rem 0 .3rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .tbl { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .9rem; background: #fff; border: 2px solid var(--ink); }
  th, td { border: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: .3rem .55rem; text-align: center; white-space: nowrap; }
  thead th { background: var(--sun); }
  tbody th { text-align: left; }
  td.strong, td.fast { background: #d6f5e0; font-weight: 800; }
  td.weak, td.slow { background: #fde0dc; font-weight: 800; }
</style>
